---
// Brand + product intent. The "how signals work" intent belongs to /javascript-signals-runtime/.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import StateNodes from "../components/StateNodes.astro";
import Install from "../components/Install.astro";
import Example from "../components/Example.astro";
import BenchStrip from "../components/BenchStrip.astro";
import ReorderCallout from "../components/ReorderCallout.astro";
import { BLOB, href } from "../site";
import { BROAD } from "../bench";
---
<Base
  path="/"
  title="Reflex Quant: Lightweight, High-Performance JavaScript Signals Library"
  headline="Reflex Quant"
  description="Reflex Quant is a tiny, high-performance JavaScript and TypeScript signals library with exact dynamic dependencies, glitch-free updates and stack-safe execution."
  faq={[
    ["What is Reflex Quant?", "Reflex Quant is a tiny, high-performance, framework-agnostic JavaScript and TypeScript signals library. It provides signals, computeds and effects with exact dynamic dependencies, glitch-free propagation, stack-safe execution and defined failure semantics."],
    ["Is Reflex Quant production ready?", "Reflex Quant is an MIT-licensed library published on npm. Its contract is differentially tested on over 140,000 programs and its performance is benchmarked in CI. It is a young library without a long public history of production deployment; evaluate its API and contract against your own requirements."],
    ["Does Reflex Quant work with React, Vue or Solid?", "Quant is framework-agnostic: it has no rendering layer. It can back state in any framework through an adapter that subscribes with effect()."],
  ]}
>
  <Hero slot="hero" />
  <p class="lede">Reflex Quant is a high-performance, framework-agnostic JavaScript and TypeScript signals library. Signals, computeds and effects fit in under 3 KB gzip, with exact dynamic dependency tracking, glitch-free propagation, stack-safe execution and defined failure semantics.</p>
  <p>In the <a href={BROAD.snapshot}>published {BROAD.date} JS Reactivity Benchmark snapshot</a>, Quant {BROAD.quantVersion} ranked first on the family-balanced core score: {BROAD.quant}× versus {BROAD.alien}× for alien-signals {BROAD.alienVersion}. This is a result for those measured workloads, not a universal speed ranking. See the <a href={href("/benchmarks/")}>benchmarks</a> and <a href={href("/alien-signals-comparison/")}>comparison</a> for scope and newer version-specific CI results.</p>
  <div id="install"><Install /></div>
  <p>Quant is a research-driven library: its runtime was derived from a fixed semantic contract and validated through differential testing. Each retained mechanism has a counterexample showing that this implementation breaks the contract or a structural bound without it. This is the smallest known set for the design, not a proven lower bound for all possible implementations.</p>

  <h2>Fine-grained reactivity in one example</h2>
  <p>A computation depends on exactly what its last run read, so dependencies change from run to run:</p>
  <Example />

  <h2>What makes Quant different</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Exact tracking.</strong> A computed observes exactly the sources it read; branches add and drop dependencies. <a href={href("/dynamic-dependency-tracking/")}>How dynamic dependency tracking works</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Glitch freedom.</strong> No computation sees a mix of old and new values, and a node runs at most once per wave, diamonds included. A write made during a run starts a new obligation (<a href={`${BLOB}/CONTRACT.md`}>P5</a>), which may run it again.</li>
    <li><strong>Stack safety.</strong> Propagation and validation use one explicit work stack, so chain depth is bounded by memory, not by the JavaScript call stack.</li>
    <li><strong>Defined failure semantics.</strong> A computed that throws commits nothing and runs again on its next demand.</li>
    <li><strong>Executable specification.</strong> Equal to the reference spec on 27,061 bounded programs, 18,660 recovery programs and 100,000 generated ones, with graph integrity checked after every operation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>A computed can own state, not just cache a derivation</h2>
  <p><code>computed(fn, initial)</code> passes <code>fn</code> its last committed value. That turns a computed into a state transition: it keeps its own state in <code>prev</code>, takes causes in through reads, and sends consequences out through writes.</p>
  <StateNodes />

  <h2>Performance</h2>
  <BenchStrip />
  <p>On a rotated read order Quant takes 0.53× Reflex's time; Reflex remains faster on reversals, random permutations and sorts (Quant takes 1.13–1.20× its time there). Against alien-signals, the difference on reorders is structural:</p>
  <ReorderCallout />
  <p>More: <a href={href("/benchmarks/")}>benchmarks and methodology</a>, <a href={href("/alien-signals-comparison/")}>alien-signals vs Reflex Quant</a>, <a href={href("/vue-reactivity-comparison/")}>Vue reactivity vs signals</a>.</p>

  <h2>Read next</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href={href("/javascript-signals-runtime/")}>JavaScript signals: what a reactive runtime actually needs</a></li>
    <li><a href={href("/reactive-runtime-anatomy/")}>Anatomy of a reactive engine</a>: how Quant is built, with diagrams</li>
    <li><a href={`${BLOB}/CONTRACT.md`}>The contract</a> (invariants and structural bounds)</li>
    <li><a href={`${BLOB}/research/LOG.md`}>Design log</a>: one counterexample per mechanism</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
